The test changes the behaviour the function comment promised, so I updated the comment along with the code:

```js
// add(a, b) returns the sum of two numbers.
// It throws TypeError when either argument is not a finite number, and
// RangeError when the sum is outside the safe integer range
// (Number.MIN_SAFE_INTEGER to Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER).
function add(a, b) {
  // Global isFinite would coerce "2" and null to numbers and accept them.
  if (!Number.isFinite(a) || !Number.isFinite(b)) {
    throw new TypeError("add: both arguments must be finite numbers");
  }
  const sum = a + b;
  if (sum > Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER || sum < Number.MIN_SAFE_INTEGER) {
    throw new RangeError("add: sum exceeds safe integer range");
  }
  return sum;
}
```

The test only checks one case, so I had to decide these:
- **It checks the range, not whether the sum is a whole number.** Using `Number.isSafeInteger(sum)` would also reject fractional results like `add(0.1, 0.2)`. Fractions inside the range still work.
- **Both directions are checked.** `add(Number.MIN_SAFE_INTEGER, -1)` throws too.
- **It throws `RangeError`.** The test only matches the message text. MDN describes `RangeError` as the error for "a value is not in the set or range of allowed values", and this keeps `TypeError` for arguments of the wrong kind. Non-finite arguments are checked first, so they still get `TypeError`.
- **Only the sum is checked, not the arguments.** That follows the test name ("results beyond the safe integer range"). So `add(1e300, -1e300)` returns 0. Rejecting out-of-range arguments as well would take one more check.

This also closes the overflow gap I mentioned last time: `add(Number.MAX_VALUE, Number.MAX_VALUE)` now throws `RangeError` instead of returning `Infinity`.

